Fillets: two roundings whose contact lines on the face between their
edges cross each other twice, the face narrowing to less than the two
roundings' width part of the way (mitcad#239). Example: a block whose two
long sides are concave arcs, the top 5.8 wide in the middle, both top
edges rounded with 4. Between the crossings the two blends cut each other
along their intersection, the face is left in two pieces, before and
after it. OCCT failed: the check of the stripes' interferences found the
contacts crossing ("fillets have too big radiuses",
ChFi3d_StripeEdgeInter), or, where 0013 had taken the contacts for
overlapping all along, the stripes computed again overflowing the face
failed or still crossed.

- trimCrossingStripes, in ChFi3d_Builder::Compute after ApplyRides: for two
  open stripes with no spine end in common (crossPartOfTheWay) whose
  contacts on one face cross twice inside both (contactsCrossTwice; two
  arcs of circles crossing come from the 2D intersection as segments of
  no length) and that ChFi3d_StripeEdgeInter would reject (no common
  corner point), the blends' intersection between the crossings is found
  (blendsMeetBetween: one blend's sections across its spine cut by the
  other blend's surface within its patch, interpolated, ending at the
  crossings; both blends are tangent to the face there, where the
  surfaces' intersection has a node). Each SurfData is split in three on
  its surface (splitSurfDataAt, one face with no section between the
  pieces, as 0144's split), the middle piece's side on the face replaced
  by the intersection, on no face, both stripes sharing that curve (as
  ApplyRides shares a contact line). The pair is not checked by
  ChFi3d_StripeEdgeInter.
- After the stripes are in the data structure (meetOnCurve): the points
  each stripe made at a crossing are made one, the curve's point
  interferences run from the first crossing to the second, and on the
  blend whose SurfData runs the other way the curve is used reversed.
- Compute, after 0013's stripes computed again overflowing a face and
  0016's rides: a pair whose contacts, as first computed, crossed twice,
  and that failed or whose contacts still cross, is computed again on the
  faces of its edges (its overlap and ride entries removed) for the cut
  above; if its contacts then do not cross twice, it fails as before.
